# Slice 302 (IDENTIFIER-CODES; the author's decision of 2026-11-07, LMX_blog/2026-11-07.md; Codex,
# CODEX-OPUS-302-IDS-HOME-SCOPE-ANSWER-20261107-01): a copy of a graph carries the codes of its occurrences' names and no
# spelling, so a copied head is recognized by its code where the copy is used.  Copy is a merge copy of Holder, a Structure
# holding two callables; a reference of Holder's model holds the copy, so the calls below select the copied callables when
# they run.  The formals of the copied f are found by the codes of their names: a named actual to the copied method, in
# swapped order, binds each formal of its name (43).  The formal x of the copied g is the nearest binding of the free input
# x of k, found by its code through the activation chain (6, not the unit's x 11): one spelling in another method has the
# same code.  The copied data field base reads by its name (100); the original f binds the same names (43).
# Entry 7; 81.. name the failing check.
int: x 11
fn: k () int
    return: x + 1
end: k
Holder:
    int: base 100
    fn: f (int: x; int: y) int
        return: x * 10 + y
    end: f
    fn: g (int: x) int
        return: k()
    end: g
end: Holder
Copy: merge: Holder
fn: named_swapped () int
    @: hc Holder Copy
    ---
        int: got hc\f(y: 3; x: 4)
        return: got
        catch: call ()
            return: 22
    ---
end: named_swapped
fn: free_through_copy () int
    @: hc Holder Copy
    return: hc\g(x: 5)
end: free_through_copy
fn: original_swapped () int
    ---
        int: got Holder\f(y: 3; x: 4)
        return: got
        catch: call ()
            return: 22
    ---
end: original_swapped
int: code 7
if: named_swapped() != 43
    code: 81
---
if: code = 7 && free_through_copy() != 6
    code: 82
---
if: code = 7 && Copy\base != 100
    code: 83
---
if: code = 7 && original_swapped() != 43
    code: 84
---
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
